#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c05d775-7693-5b44-9b77-3a537e7cc430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276001} {:answer/id #uuid "90bf3216-1795-5dd4-84c1-b977a28bad0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276002} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa2b771-ea79-5f77-8eec-35f63e4bb977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276003} {:answer/id #uuid "e086b96b-1b4f-5e23-9ec3-d0c9607006f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276004} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2517b1-51f0-58dd-9e23-8c4b5de0d0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa2b771-ea79-5f77-8eec-35f63e4bb977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276003} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9ae66b-6772-58a3-a25b-84917c1a1a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb3f02cc-ae8b-5d22-af77-c5a4f4e69173" :question/math-academy-id "q-107562" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca64ca4a-1994-599a-86c3-cc5f7134988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.741)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276008} {:answer/id #uuid "210f4b85-e971-5daf-af1b-c80a09dfd2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.841)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276009} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c3630-b06a-5eaf-93e7-3f73d1bf9a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.841)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276010} {:answer/id #uuid "91f096e9-4a32-58f2-b77c-743c4f86df53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276011} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa7b1b1-262f-5f6f-85d7-d9cf27a72f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f096e9-4a32-58f2-b77c-743c4f86df53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276011} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b3225e-802b-5dcf-888b-3a423420e7b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "016733e0-c899-5c32-9163-e5388a18d61b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107574" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.730\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95528d27-c43e-59c3-bbfd-8fd5e06bfc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95528d27-c43e-59c3-bbfd-8fd5e06bfc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276015} :answer-field/id #uuid "b380ce15-728c-52bd-882e-468ca1776de4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d51e50a-143e-5477-be9d-b3394c15a0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d51e50a-143e-5477-be9d-b3394c15a0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b2e1eb-9698-563f-8755-97414cd6b848" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "383887c5-bb2b-5b2e-b8c0-e85d6d548c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327988" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})≈1.061\\text{ }\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)≈\\sqrt{21}\\,sin\\,(6x-1.061).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2726a2e-489b-51dd-990c-aefd7189dd43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276020} {:answer/id #uuid "b70b4a52-6461-5642-a5fe-aee5e2e51693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276021} {:answer/id #uuid "9969be14-ed57-529f-b3f1-c4d6b54604ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a286e4-c84a-58de-b270-c54eddb95ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276023} {:answer/id #uuid "8250ef2f-7289-5956-b472-73327e0a40bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9969be14-ed57-529f-b3f1-c4d6b54604ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} :answer-field/id #uuid "98df750c-d872-5b24-ad78-2b70b57bf185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1233738d-9980-5519-b543-2a6375658840" :question/math-academy-id "q-65574" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9cb234-eed4-507c-9ec1-9e9aceafae02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276027} {:answer/id #uuid "c6843a56-169c-5c75-8868-e9c86cab9108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276028} {:answer/id #uuid "82f6df96-b111-5419-bd2a-259f3d9437cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276029} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfff3f8-100c-55a5-a281-9de36806e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276030} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dbb64a-3cac-5b76-afcb-707ed66a1feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfff3f8-100c-55a5-a281-9de36806e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276030} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed077ed-e31f-5fb4-aff3-0324418e805a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2482eaaf-f514-5577-a783-2f1ca414760f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80135" :question/problem "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
